---
import IllustrationFigure from "~/components/illustration-kit/IllustrationFigure.astro"
import SvgFigure from "~/components/illustration-kit/SvgFigure.astro"
import { series, stroke } from "~/data/palette"
---

<IllustrationFigure
  name="dalemberts-paradox"
  lead="The ideal flow has no wake. The real flow does."
>
  <SvgFigure
    name="dalembert-flows"
    class="mx-auto w-full max-w-lg"
    viewBox="0 0 400 340"
    title="Symmetric ideal flow compared with separated real flow"
    desc="Above, smooth streamlines bend equally around the front and back of a stationary round body in an ideal frictionless flow. Below, separated streamlines remain apart behind the body, enclosing a broad shaded red wake region with a small eddy. Flow moves from left to right in both panels. Schematic, not a measured flow field."
  >
    <!-- Ideal flow is symmetric in front of and behind the body. -->
    <text x="12" y="21" fill="currentColor" font-size="17" font-weight="700">
      Ideal flow: front and back match
    </text>
    <path
      d="M20 36 H62 m-6 -5 6 5 -6 5"
      fill="none"
      stroke={series.blue}
      stroke-width="2"
      stroke-linecap="round"
      stroke-linejoin="round"
    />
    <g fill="none" stroke={series.blue} stroke-width="2">
      <path d="M12 48 H388" />
      <path d="M12 79 C106 79 123 61 200 61 S294 79 388 79" />
      <path d="M12 127 C106 127 123 145 200 145 S294 127 388 127" />
      <path d="M12 158 H388" />
    </g>
    <circle
      cx="200"
      cy="103"
      r="30"
      fill={series.gold}
      fill-opacity=".25"
      stroke={stroke}
      stroke-width="2"
    />

    <!-- Lower panel is shifted down so its heading clears the upper flow. -->
    <g transform="translate(0 20)">
      <text x="12" y="180" fill="currentColor" font-size="17" font-weight="700">
        Real flow: separation and a wake
      </text>
      <path
        d="M20 199 H62 m-6 -5 6 5 -6 5"
        fill="none"
        stroke={series.blue}
        stroke-width="2"
        stroke-linecap="round"
        stroke-linejoin="round"
      />

      <!-- The shaded wake stays at least as wide as the body downstream. -->
      <path
        d="M230 226 C276 217 330 218 388 220 L388 286 C330 288 276 287 230 280 Z"
        fill={series.red}
        fill-opacity=".15"
      />
      <!-- The outer streamlines do not close back together behind the body. -->
      <g fill="none" stroke={series.blue} stroke-width="2">
        <path d="M12 232 C112 232 143 218 185 216 C205 214 218 217 234 221 C271 225 324 217 388 217" />
        <path d="M12 274 C112 274 143 288 185 290 C205 292 218 289 234 285 C271 281 324 289 388 289" />
      </g>
      <circle
        cx="200"
        cy="253"
        r="30"
        fill={series.gold}
        fill-opacity=".25"
        stroke={stroke}
        stroke-width="2"
      />
      <path
        d="M267 253 C280 242 300 244 305 254 C312 270 288 273 282 260"
        fill="none"
        stroke={series.red}
        stroke-width="2"
        stroke-linecap="round"
      />
    </g>
  </SvgFigure>
  <Fragment slot="caption">
    Without viscosity the idealised steady flow has front-back symmetry and predicts zero drag.
    In real fluids, viscosity matters near the surface and the flow can separate, leaving a
    low-pressure wake that creates drag. Schematic, not to scale.
  </Fragment>
</IllustrationFigure>
